{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Core Sections in an ASP.NET Page "}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about controls and core sections in ASP.NET."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand information about web applications, their principles and basic concepts in a range of work contexts.  Describe applications for an operating system for personal computers in a range of work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on web applications, and applications for operating systems for personal computers."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "validation control "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a mechanism to authenticate user input data "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A page has different types; for example, HTML and Web server controls, validation controls and user controls."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "inheritance"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a model in computing programming in which an object or class inherits the features of another"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Are these controls run by inheritance? "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "core "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "essential or primary"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I'll show you how to recognize the core sections in the following code snippet."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "attribute "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a particular condition that specifies the properties of an HTML element"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This is done through different page-specific attributes that allow importing namespaces, loading assemblies or registering new controls."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "event handler "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a sequence of code or routine used to solve a request"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This is considered as an event that needs a coded answer from the event handler. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "interface "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "graphical resources that allow users to interact within a page or application"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, it is directly linked to the interface."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob03aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Find out more information about sections on ASP.NET pages. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Identify the sections on a code snippet. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a slider tutorial to explain each section on your code snippet."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare an oral presentation to present your slider tutorial.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your work with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find out more information about sections on ASP.NET pages. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I identify the sections on a code snippet.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a slider tutorial to explain each section on my code snippet.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I prepare an oral presentation to present my slider tutorial.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my work with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "WH Word Clauses + Verb: How to, What to and Where to "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We can use clauses with a <strong>wh word</strong> and a verb when we are using verbs of thinking or saying such as know, tell, suggest or ask. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>How to </strong></br></br>  We use <strong>how to + verb</strong> to emphasize on the manner in which an action is done. For example: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>The page directives know <strong>how to set up</strong> the environment.</i> </br></br>  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'll show you how to recognize the core sections in the following code snippet. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Each one of those sections is supposed to show you what to use as attributes."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I will tell you later where to find more information about this topic in case you have any doubts."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>What to</strong></br></br>  We use <strong>what to + verb </strong>when we are referring to the possible answer to a specific question. For example: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>Programmer's tutorials always suggest <strong>what to do</strong> in case of having trouble when setting up controls. </i></br></br>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Where to</strong> </br></br>  We use <strong>where to + verb </strong>to highlight the space where something can take place. For example: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>The professor told us <strong>where to </strong>find interesting tutorials about ASP.NET. </i></br></br>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": " This is the structure that you generally use with<strong> WH word clauses + verb</strong>: </br></br> <center>  Subject + verb + <strong>wh word+ to + verb in base form </strong>+ complement.</center>  </br></br>   Examples: </br> \u2022\t<i>The code section shows <strong>how to</strong> provide handlers for the page control. </br> \u2022\tYou need to know <strong>how to design </strong>the page layout. </br> \u2022\tHe suggested us <strong>what to do</strong> to design our app.  </i>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Passive in Interrogative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <strong>simple present passive</strong> in questions when you need to ask how things are supposed to be on a regular basis."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When we ask questions in active voice, we aim to highlight the person or element that performs an action: </br></br>  \u2022<i>\tDo <strong>users</strong> save ASP.NET pages with the .aspx extension? </i></br></br>  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Are ASP.NET pages made up of only controls?  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Are these controls run by inheritance?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Is an ASP.NET page divided into core sections?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "But when it comes to highlighting the role of the object in a sentence, we need to use the passive voice. We also used this tense in questions to confirm that something is done the way it is supposed to: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>Are  <strong>ASP.NET pages</strong> saved with the .aspx extension? </br> \u2022\tAre <strong>ASP.NET pages</strong> with the .aspx extension saved by users ? </i> </br></br>  When using the passive voice the subject could be omitted, but in case you wish to mention it, you need to use the particle <strong>by </strong>followed by the noun. </br></br>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "This is the grammatical structure we need to use when building questions in <strong>simple present passive</strong>: </br></br> <center>  <strong>Verb be </strong>+ Object+ <strong>Verb in past participle </strong>+ Complement?</center>  </br></br>   Example:  </br></br>  \u2022\t<i><strong>Are</strong> page-specific attributes <strong>specified </strong>by directives in ASP.NET?</i>"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "contents": [], "type": "video", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Martha</strong>: Before starting the exam, we will revise some definitions. So, do you have any questions? </br><strong>Freddy</strong>: I have one! Are ASP.NET pages made up of only controls? </br><strong>Martha: </strong> Sort of.  To be more precise I can tell you that controls in ASP.NET pages are essential. A page has different types; for example, HTML and Web server controls, validation controls, and user controls. </br><strong>Gloria</strong>: Are these controls run by inheritance? </br><strong>Martha: </strong> Yes, they are. As all ASP.NET elements, controls are objects that usually inherit functions from a parental control class.</br>"}, {"time": "46000", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Freddy: </strong>I have another question. Is an ASP.NET page divided into sections? </br><strong>Martha</strong>: Indeed! ASP.NET pages are modular.  I'll show you how to recognize the core sections in the following code snippet. Here we have: first, the Page Directives, the Code Section, and finally the Page Layout.  Each one of those sections is supposed to show you what to use as an attribute. </br><strong>Freddy</strong>: But, are those attributes established independently for each core section? </br><strong>Gloria</strong>: I think so. For example, page directives are the ones that create an environment for making a page run.  These directives are in charge of determining how to process pages. This is done through different page-specific attributes that allow importing namespaces, loading assemblies or registering new controls. </br><strong>Martha</strong>: You're right Gloria. Freddy, could you help us explaining Code Section?  </br>"}, {"time": "107000", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Freddy</strong>: I know that Code Section is the one that makes handlers and control events available in a page. For example, every time that a user clicks a button to request something, this is considered as an event that needs a coded answer from the event handler.  </br><strong>Martha</strong>: That's certainly true. Let's finish with Page Layout. What can you tell us about that Gloria? </br><strong>Gloria: </strong> Well, it is directly linked to the interface. This section has different elements such as server controls, HTML tags, and inline JavaScript. </br><strong>Martha: </strong> OK. I think you are all set. Let's start with the test!     I will tell you later where to find more information about this topic in case you have any doubts. </br>"}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le01ob03re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le01ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 2, "title": ""}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": "", "type": "img"}], "type": "static", "template_override": 1, "id": 3, "title": ""}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_dat